et's decide 1 = common mundane boring. That fits.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). Stated problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources... Proposal ensures funding for log storage etc. Directly applies. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem. 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Does budget line solve logging mandate? It ensures resources allocated, but doesn't implement logging technically. It contributes to solution but not solve technical logging. Effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). It addresses funding risk, not actual logging capability. So 3.\n\nWe need output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating... For each subscale give ... Provide evidence first then score.\n\nLet's produce structured output:\n\nid: P1911\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 4\netc.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nImplementability evidence: Revising a grant-review template and requiring PI/finance sign-off on a logging budget line is an administrative change that can be enacted quickly with minimal cost. Score 4.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Requiring explicit compliance funding aligns with regulatory expectations and standard financial controls, offending no norms. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal adds a budgetary process element but still frames compliance as separate logging practice rather than redefining R&D-compliance relations. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: Dedicated compliance budget lines in project charters are a com