. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. So 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): common strategies violate no norms =4. Somewhat uncommon unusual but not offend =3. Offends sensibilities somewhat=2. Radically violates=1.\n\nMarketing compliance docs to clients is socially/legal/politically acceptable. Common strategies. So 4.\n\nNeed format: for each subscale, give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProvide id echo.\n\nOutput probably JSON? Probably lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2987\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft succinct sentences.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal creates a client-facing audit package that turns compliance documentation into a sales differentiator, changing how compliance obligations relate to R&D outputs.\nScore 3.\n\noriginality_evidence: Bundling technical docs and logs as a marketable deliverable is an interesting repackaging of standard compliance work but not surprising or ingenious.\nScore 2.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: It will improve lifetime documentation maintenance and traceability but does not itself build the mandatory automatic event logging required by Article 12.\nScore 3.\n\napplicability_evidence: The package directly addresses Recital 71’s clear-and-comprehensive documentation and lifetime-maintenance obligation central to meeting the logging mandate.\nScore 4.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Templating a document set per product family and refreshing it at major releases is reasonably feasible with compliance and sales engineering resources.\nScore 3.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Offering audit-ready documentation as a value-add is a common, norm-compliant business practice that does not offend legal or market sensibilities.\nScore 4.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
