nd Y is part of stated problem)\nProposal directly addresses meeting logging mandate. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\nThey say small team builds schema layer on top existing tracking infrastructure, near-zero additional marginal engineering cost. So seems easy? Might be some changes. I'd say 3 or 4. \"easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\". Schema layer is some change but low cost. Could be 4. I'll give 3? The description says near-zero additional marginal engineering cost. That suggests easy. I'll go 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\nThis is compliant and improves reproducibility. Acceptable. 4.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nCompliance logs as scientific artefacts is somewhat unusual but not extremely novel? Might be 3. Originality: converting compliance burden into publication-worthy contribution. That's unusual interesting. Probably 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice\n\nProposal weaves logging into science, makes logs first-class scientific artefacts. That changes 