ree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nAutomated compliance gate in CI for regulatory logging. This is fairly common practice: contract tests for compliance. Might be interesting but not ingenious. Probably originality 2? Or 3? I'd say it's interesting but not rare. Many labs do CI checks. Could be 2 = interesting. Let's choose 2.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nGate introduces new element (automated conformance test) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching. So 2.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale, give <subscale>_evidence first then <subscale>. Probably lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nP1865\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nProceed.\n\n",
