        "content": "{\n  \"ratings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1878\",\n      \"effectiveness_evidence\": \"The proposal markets existing logging rather than building or adapting the automatic event recording mandated by Article 12 and Recital 71.\",\n      \"effectiveness\": 1,\n      \"applicability_evidence\": \"It addresses tender competitiveness instead of the core R&D decision of how to meet the logging mandate and adapt R&D accordingly.\",\n      \"applicability\": 2,\n      \"paradigm_relatedness_evidence\": \"It keeps compliance as an external reporting exercise and does not weave logging into R&D or change what the lab researches.\",\n      \"paradigm_relatedness\": 2,\n      \"originality_evidence\": \"Framing regulatory compliance as a sales differentiator with a mapping dossier is a common, mundane business practice.\",\n      \"originality\": 1,\n      \"acceptability_evidence\": \"Publicising verified Article 12 readiness in EU tenders complies with legal norms and does not offend sensibilities.\",\n      \"acceptability\": 4,\n      \"implementability_evidence\": \"Creating a one-page compliance dossier per product and attaching it to tenders requires only coordination with business development and low-cost documentation.\",\n      \"implementability\": 4\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rating for proposal P1878.\n\nProposal: Market Repositioning Around Compliance as a Feature. Package Article 12 logging as selling point, produce one-page compliance dossier mapping logged field to sub-article, include in tender.\n\nRate six subscales.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore...\n\nDoes marketing repositioning solve logging mandate? It does not implement logging; it assumes systems already meet Act's record-keeping minimums out of box. So effectiveness? The idea is about market repositioning, not actually meeting logging. If systems already meet, then it's just documentation. But proposal is to package as selling point and produce dossier. That does not ensure technical logging capability is built. It contributes partially to compliance via documentation? Might be partial solution.\n\nEffectiveness definition: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps, but only partial). 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. 1 = solves unrelated problem.\n\nDoes market repositioning solve logging mandate? No. It assumes logging already exists. So effectiveness is low. Probably 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve the problem? Or 1 = solves unrelated problem (marketing). The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Marketing does not meet mandate, it's about sales. Might be solving unrelated problem of market share.\n\nI think effectiveness = 2? Unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. It won't make logs happen. Could be 1 solves unrelated problem. Let's interpret: It would not work to meet logging mandate even if you could do it. The proposal does not address building logs. So it solves an unrelated problem (competitive differentiation). So effectiveness =1.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = intervention not stated or does not produce useful outcome\n\nIt maps logged field to sub-article for tender. That's related to compliance readiness, but not core logging implementation. Might be 2? May have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates. Or 3 solves implied problem? Implied problem is verifying supplier readiness. Hmm.\n\nThe stated problem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. This proposal adapts business development, not R&D. It somehow relates. I'd say applicability =2 (may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates). Could be 3 solves implied problem that is related: ensuring clients trust compliance? Might be 3.\n\nLet's pick applicability=2? Let's think: does X produce Y part of stated problem? X = produce dossier and market as feature. Y = meet logging mandate? No. So not direct. It's about tendering. Somehow relates to compliance obligations. I'd say 2.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposal turns regulatory obligation into competitive differentiator, produce dossier. That's new element? It's marketing. Does it change relationship between R&D and compliance? No, R&D unchanged. It just reframes compliance. Probably slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements (compliance dossier as sales tool) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Maybe paradigm_relatedness =2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare and ingenious.\n4 not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 interesting\n1 common, mundane, boring\n\nMarket repositioning around compliance as feature is common strategy. Mundane. Originality=1.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 common strategies violate no norms\n3 somewhat uncommon but not offend\n2 offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 radically violates laws\n\nMarketing compliance readiness is acceptable. 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 some changes, reasonably feasible\n2 significant change, expensive/difficult\n1 totally infeasible\n\nProduce one-page dossier per product and attach to tender from next quarter. Coordinate with business development. Easy low cost. So 4.\n\nNow produce output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nOrder listed: effectiveness, applicability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide evidence one sentence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences:\n\neffectiveness_evidence: The proposal markets existing logging rather than building or adapting the automatic event recording mandated by Article 12 and Recital 71.\neffectiveness: 1\n\napplicability_evidence: It addresses tender competitiveness instead of the core R&D decision of how to meet the logging mandate.\napplicability: 2? Could be 2.\n\nMaybe applicability 3? Let's reconsider definition: solves implied problem related to stated problem (do X to get implied Y, which applies). Implied problem could be demonstrating compliance to clients. That is related to stated problem? Hmm.\n\nThe stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Demonstrating readiness is part of adapting? Maybe 3.\n\nI think applicability =2 is safer: may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It keeps compliance as an external reporting exercise and does not weave logging into R&D or change what the lab researches.\nparadigm_relatedness: 2? Slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements tools formats processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. The dossier is new element. So 2.\n\noriginality_evidence: Framing regulatory compliance as a sales differentiator with a mapping dossier is a common, mundane business practice.\noriginality:1\n\nacceptability_evidence: Publicising verified Article 12 readiness in EU tenders complies with legal norms and does not offend sensibilities.\nacceptability:4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Creating a one-page compliance dossier per product and attaching it to tenders requires only coordination with business development and low-cost documentation.\nimplementability:4\n\nReturn rating.\n\nLet's output JSON? Probably plain. Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\"... then \"<subscale>\".\n\nI'll output structured lines.\n\n",
