ear simulation. Probably significant change/expensive => 2. Might be 3 reasonably feasible? For an AI R&D lab committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal. Implementation is research project, not logging mandate fulfillment. Could be difficult. I think 2.\n\nApplicability: 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Stated problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nDoes open benchmark solve meeting logging mandate? No, it doesn't implement logging for their own systems. It is about evaluating logging fidelity generally. Applies to problem? Might be implied problem: improve logging capabilities? Does X get Y which applies to stated problem. The proposal does not directly meet logging mandate for their own high-risk AI systems. It may contribute to understanding logging but not directly solve compliance.\n\nApplicability scale: 4 solves identified problem directly related. 3 solves implied problem related. 2 may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates. 1 intervention not stated.\n\nI think it may have some benefit within special situation (if they use benchmark to improve own logs) and somehow relates. Might be 2. Or 3 solves implied problem: improve logging practice? Hmm.\n\nThe stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. The proposal adapts R&D to focus on logging evaluation methodology, which is adapting R&D accordingly. So it applies. Could be applicability 3? Solves implied problem that is related: build expertise/community around logging compliance.\n\nI think applicability = 3? Let's interpret: do X (benchmark) to get Y (methodological conversation). Y is part of stated problem? Stated problem is meet logging mand