        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence evidence.\n\nProposal: Ring-Fence Fifteen Percent of Project Budgets for Compliance Engineering...\n\nWe need rate six subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm, anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is budget ring-fence. Does it change relationship? It's still treating compliance as logging practice, just budgeting. Introduces new element (budget rule). Slightly paradigm stretching? Probably introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nBudget ring-fence 15% for compliance engineering. Is that common? Probably common practice in regulated industries. Might be mundane. Could be common, mundane, boring => 1. Maybe interesting? Budget reserve is standard. I'd say 1 = common, mundane.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Approval means committing scarce resources. Ring-fencing budget ensures resources for logging. Will it solve? It contributes to ability to meet mandate by ensuring funds, but doesn't actually implement logs. So contributes partially. Reasonable and will contribute to solution. That's 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1 = ...\n\nDo X = ring-fence budget, get Y = resources for logging infrastructure. Y is part of stated problem: meeting logging mandate. So directly related. 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nBudget reserve is acceptable. Common strategy. No violation. 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\n1 = totally infeasible...\n\nBaking into standard funding template starting next quarter. That is some change but reasonably feasible. Might be 3? Easy to implement at low cost? Changing budget template may require committee approval, but seems non-radical. Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. I think it's somewhat change: need committee. Maybe 3.\n\nLet's choose implementability = 3 (some changes, reasonably feasible). Might be 4. Hmm.\n\nStandard funding template change is moderate. I'd say 3.\n\nNow produce output with evidence sentences then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction say