        "content": "{\"ratings\": [{\"id\":\"P2926\",\"effectiveness_evidence\":\"Standardizing log schemas to capture use periods, reference database versions, input matches and verifier identities directly satisfies Article 12(1)-(3) logging mandates while creating a research use for those logs.\",\"effectiveness\":4,\"applicability_evidence\":\"The proposal explicitly targets automatic event logging and Annex III data fields required by Article 12, mapping directly onto the mandate to meet logging requirements and adapt R&D.\",\"applicability\":4,\"paradigm_relatedness_evidence\":\"It reframes mandatory compliance logging as a core scientific asset, changing the relationship between the lab’s R&D work and its compliance obligations.\",\"paradigm_relatedness\":4,\"originality_evidence\":\"Treating required compliance logs as a proprietary longitudinal behavioral corpus for model drift research is unusual for AI labs and shows imagination beyond routine filing.\",\"originality\":3,\"implementability_evidence\":\"Partnering with data engineering to unify schemas across Annex III deployments is reasonably feasible but requires cross-team coordination and schema changes.\",\"implementability\":3,\"acceptability_evidence\":\"Reusing logs containing personal data for internal research is somewhat uncommon and needs privacy governance, but does not radically violate laws or sensibilities.\",\"acceptability\":3}]\n\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Need rating.\n\nProposal: Compliance Logs as Behavioral Research Corpus. Reframe logs into longitudinal research dataset... standardise schemas... turns compliance cost into scientific asset.\n\nWe need effectiveness, applicability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, implementability, acceptability.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: one sentence then score.\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: lab must decide how to respond: meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nWill reframing logs into research corpus solve problem of meeting logging mandate? It helps meet logging mandate by standardizing schemas to capture required events. Also adapts R&D. Does it solve stated problem without regard for workability? Probably will contribute to solution, not fully solve mandate? It addresses logging mandate and adaptation. Might be reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Effectiveness 4 would be solve main problem without regard workability. Does it solve logging mandate? Standardizing schemas ensures logs capture timestamps, input matches... That's required by Article 12 3(a)-(d). So it seems directly meets mandate and adds research value. Might be 4? \"will solve the stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve the main problem)\". If you could do it, would it solve logging mandate? Yes, plus adapt R&D. Could be 4.\n\nBut maybe effectiveness is about meeting logging mandate and adapting R&D. Turning logs into research corpus does not by itself guarantee compliance; still need technical logging capability. But standardising schemas helps. I'd say reasonable and will contribute... Hmm.\n\nLet's interpret: problem is decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal addresses both. It would solve? Might be partial because research use doesn't ensure logs are automatically recorded per Article 12(1). But standardisation does help.\n\nI'll give effectiveness = 3? The anchored 4 = will solve stated problem without regard for workability. If you could do it, it would solve main problem. I think it would indeed solve main problem: meet mandate and adapt R&D. Might be 4.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related. Do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Yes: standardize logs to meet Article 12 requirements and use for research. That's directly related. So applicability 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This does change relationship: compliance becomes research asset. Introduces new elements. Might be paradigm stretching? Paradigm breaking requires changes relationship OR addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It does change relationship between R&D and compliance obligations - compliance becomes source of scientific asset. That's paradigm breaking? Definition: introduces new elements AND changes the relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Yes it changes relationship. Could be 4.\n\nOriginality: 4 = not expressed before (rare, unusual) AND ingenious... Is this rare? Using compliance logs as research corpus may be unusual but maybe interesting. Might be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Not sure if ingenious. I'd say 3.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented. Standardising log schemas across Annex III deployments, partner with data engineering... That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Might be some changes. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability: socially legally politically acceptable. Using logs for research - need consent? Logs contain input data for which search led to match, identification of natural persons involved in verification. That is personal data. Using for research may raise privacy/legal issues. But proposal says standardise schemas for statistical analysis rather than mere regulatory filing. Might be acceptable? Could be somewhat uncommon but does not offend sensibilities. Maybe 3? Or 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Using logs for internal research might be acceptable with safeguards. I'd say 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities.\n\nLet's produce output with evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale in order listed: FIRST write one sentence of evidence for that subscale (your reasoning before the number). THEN give its score as integer level.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Could be plain text.\n\nI'll produce structured list.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Standardizing schemas to capture timestamps, reference database versions, verifier annotations directly satisfies Article 12(1)-(3) logging requirements while repurposing logs for drift research. Score 4.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal explicitly targets automatic event logging and Annex III data fields mandated by Article 12, so it maps one-to-one onto the compliance and R&D adaptation problem. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It reframes mandatory compliance logging as a core scientific asset, thereby changing how R&D and regulatory obligations relate. Score 4.\n\nOriginality evidence: Treating required compliance logs as a proprietary longitudinal behavioral corpus is unusual for labs and shows imagination beyond routine filing. Score 3.\n\nImplementability evidence: Partnering with data engineering to unify schemas is feasible but requires cross-team coordination and schema changes across deployments. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Reusing logged personal data for internal research is uncommon and requires privacy safeguards, but does not violate laws or sensibilities if properly governed. Score 3.\n\nLet's output.\n\n",
