        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Need rating.\n\nProposal: Internal Logging Middleware for High-Risk Builds. Shared version-controlled logging library integrated at build time, capturing timestamps, reference-database pointers, matched inputs, verifier identities in single schema. Two platform engineers prototype over one quarter within existing CI/CD; project leads mandate integration at sprint zero for any Annex III system. One shared component amortises fixed cost...\n\nWe need subscales:\n\nacceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nEvidence sentence then score.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms? Logging middleware is common compliance. Should be 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Probably 4.\n\nimplementability: easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Two platform engineers prototype over one quarter within existing CI/CD; mandate integration. Some changes, reasonably feasible? Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Or 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. It's shared component amortises cost. Might be 3? It requires building library and mandating integration. Maybe \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\" = 3. Could be 4? Hmm low cost? Two engineers one quarter is moderate. I'd say 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice