e only covers Article12(3) fields, not all Article12(2). But still effective. Probably 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem. If you could do it, it would solve main problem of meeting logging mandate.\n\nMaybe effectiveness 4.\n\nProvide output with id and each subscale evidence then score.\n\nFormat: Return rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Might be lines. Probably produce mapping.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2795\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The SDK introduces a reusable middleware and fixed schema but continues to treat compliance as mandated logging rather than redefining how the lab conducts R&D.\nScore 2\n\noriginality_evidence: Shared instrumentation libraries for regulatory logging are a common software engineering practice and the proposal offers no surprising or ingenious twist.\nScore 1\n\nacceptability_evidence: Using encrypted middleware to capture required logs aligns with legal requirements and data protection norms and offends no sensibilities.\nScore 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building and maintaining a lab-wide SDK with per-architecture hooks requires coordinated engineering effort beyond trivial changes.\nScore 3\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly targets the Article 12 mandate to automatically record session times, reference databases, matches, and verifier identity for high-risk prototypes.\nScore 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: If implemented, automatic capture of the Annex III logging fields would meet the core traceability requirement without per-project rework.\nScore 4\n\nMake sure order listed: paradigm_relatedness, originality, acceptability, implementability, applicability, effectiveness.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
