tion for all high-risk systems, just biometric prototypes. Might be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could be 4 if solves stated problem without regard workability. The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This gives a baseline for biometric teams, eliminating redundant design. Seems will solve part of problem for that subclass. Probably 3 = contributes.\n\nEvidence: It delivers schema-conformant logs for prototyping but does not guarantee ongoing lifetime logging, post-market monitoring, or non-biometric high-risk systems.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\nReference implementation with fixtures is common practice. Probably common, mundane. Score 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Reference modules and synthetic test fixtures are standard engineering practice for compliance schema adoption.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nIt introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Score 2.\n\nEvidence: It adds a shared reference module and fixtures to the R&D workflow yet keeps compliance framed as implementing required log fields rather than redefining research.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Publishing an internal reference implementation using synthetic data complies with law and does not offend sensibilities.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nTwo engineers build it in four weeks on standard serving stack; seems easy low cost, or non-radical changes. Score 4 = easy to implement at low cost.\n\nEvidence: The plan specifies two engineers for four weeks on existing stack wit