actice. Shared service for compliance logging is fairly common, maybe interesting but mundane. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 interesting? Might be common. Let's say 1.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\nProposal directly addresses logging mandate: captures Article12(3) data points automatically. So solves identified problem directly. 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nWill unified platform capture logs? Yes, it would solve logging mandate for high-risk deployments. Seems reasonable. Score 4.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nShared logging service is acceptable. 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\nAllocate one senior engineer for two sprints to design schema and integrate into containerised deployment. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be 3. Could be 4? Easy to implement at low cost? One senior engineer two sprints is not trivial but low cost relative. Might be 3. Let's say 3.\n\nNeed evidence one sentence each.\n\nOutput format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON?\n\nReturn rating of proposal, echoing its id exactly. Provide fields.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1296\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\netc.\n\nEnsure order listed: paradigm_relatedness, originality, applicability, effectiveness, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal introduces 